/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The analyte is the substance being measured: glucose in blood, carbon monoxide in air, nitrate in water.
   - The recognition element is the part that binds or reacts with one analyte and largely ignores the rest.
   - The transducer converts the recognition event into a voltage, a current, a resistance or a light level that a circuit can read.
   - Classification uses two independent axes.
   - Electrochemical sensors are the largest family.
   - I is the output current in nA; I₀ is the blank offset current in nA (the reading at zero analyte); S is the sensitivity in nA per mmol/L; C is the analyte concentration in mmol/L.
   - Optical sensors use absorbance (more analyte absorbs more light) or fluorescence (the analyte or a label emits light).
   - An enzyme sensor uses one enzyme that catalyses one reaction.
   - An immunosensor uses an antibody that binds one antigen, the molecule it was raised against.
   - A microbial or whole-cell sensor uses living cells as the recognition element.
*/
