/* book exam traps: Using log₁₀ instead of ln in the Nernst equation. For a ratio of 10.45 at 973 K, log₁₀ gives 1.019 and an EMF of 21.4 mV instead of 49.2 mV. | Putting the temperature in degrees Celsius. At 700 °C, T = 973 K; using 700 in the formula gives 35.4 mV instead of 49.2 mV. | Dropping the 4 in 4F for the four-electron oxygen reaction. That makes the EMF four times too large. | Reversing the sign: the electrode with more oxygen is positive, so the reference air electrode is positive when the exhaust is oxygen-poor. | Thinking a chemiresistor's resistance rises for a reducing gas. It falls, because the gas returns trapped electrons to the SnO₂ film. | Confusing paramagnetic oxygen sensing with thermal-conductivity hydrogen sensing. Both are physical methods, but they exploit different properties and different gases. */
